WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:03.420
In Search of Dharma.

2
00:00:05.493 --> 00:00:08.120
3: The Dharmas of the Samin of Java.

3
00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:12.600
An illiterate farmer, a fenced-off forest,

4
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:16.940
and a way of living so complete it
outlasted its maker by a hundred years.

5
00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:19.820
What a Javanese peasant movement can show
us

6
00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:22.620
that the great religions no longer easily
can.

7
00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:27.360
Sometime in the first years of the
twentieth century,

8
00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:29.800
on a dirt road in the teak country of
central Java,

9
00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:34.540
a Dutch colonial official stops a barefoot
farmer and asks him a routine question.

10
00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:37.220
Where are you from?

11
00:00:37.698 --> 00:00:39.860
The farmer answers: from the forest.

12
00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:41.420
Where are you going?

13
00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:42.940
To the forest.

14
00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:45.840
Every word is true; every word is useless.

15
00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:49.200
The official has learned nothing he can
write in a report,

16
00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:51.100
and nothing he can punish,

17
00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:53.560
because the man has not refused to answer;

18
00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:56.700
he has answered perfectly, and said
nothing at all.

19
00:00:56.980 --> 00:01:01.420
The scene comes down to us as Javanese
memory rather than colonial paperwork --

20
00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:03.740
no archive holds this exchange --

21
00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:06.620
but it is how the tradition itself tells
the story, and,

22
00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:10.860
as we will see, it is exactly how the
archive shows these people talking.

23
00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:15.700
If you were that official, you would know
you were being mocked.

24
00:01:16.239 --> 00:01:18.460
You would also know you could not prove
it.

25
00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:23.060
And if you were patient enough to keep
asking (and the Dutch, by every account,

26
00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:26.020
asked and asked) you would slowly realise
this

27
00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:28.580
was not one stubborn man having a bad
morning.

28
00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:30.200
It was a method.

29
00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:34.460
A whole community, thousands of people
across a cluster of villages,

30
00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:37.000
had quietly agreed to meet the colonial
state

31
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:39.620
with this same deadpan, literal
politeness.

32
00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:43.720
They paid no tax they could avoid,
registered no marriages,

33
00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:45.700
and answered the census with riddles.

34
00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:48.540
And they did it all without ever raising a
hand.

35
00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:52.740
A hundred years later, in 2017,

36
00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:55.300
nine women farmers travelled from those
same hills

37
00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:58.060
to the gates of the Presidential Palace in
Jakarta,

38
00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:01.660
sat down on the pavement, and poured wet
cement around their feet.

39
00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:06.220
They stayed there for hours, locked to the
ground they had come to argue about,

40
00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:08.520
while the traffic of the capital flowed
past.

41
00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:11.200
One of them, a woman named Patmi,

42
00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:14.500
forty-eight years old, died not long after
the protest.

43
00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:17.620
They were the heirs of the man on the
forest road:

44
00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:20.520
some by blood, all by their manner of
refusal.

45
00:02:22.175 --> 00:02:26.000
So I went looking for what connected them,
and what I found was a dharma.

46
00:02:29.024 --> 00:02:31.000
A dharma you can hold in your hand

47
00:02:33.252 --> 00:02:36.540
The story so far has been very large and
very abstract:

48
00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:40.040
civilisations, millennia, the Axial Age.

49
00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:42.000
I wanted the opposite.

50
00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:48.240
I wanted a dharma I could almost touch:
small enough to see all the way around,

51
00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:51.360
recent enough that we know the names of
the people who built it,

52
00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:55.560
and humble enough that no one had spent
two thousand years polishing it

53
00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:57.520
into something grand and untouchable.

54
00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:02.540
Not a world religion with a billion
followers, in other words.

55
00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:04.380
Something hand-made.

56
00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:08.320
The Samin of Java are exactly that.

57
00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:12.720
Their dharma was assembled, more or less
in front of the historical record,

58
00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:16.580
by a single farmer and his neighbours,
beginning around 1890.

59
00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:19.880
We know roughly when it started, what set
it off,

60
00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:22.280
what it asked of people, and how it
spread.

61
00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:24.440
We can watch it being made.

62
00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:27.060
And because we can watch it being made,

63
00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:29.920
it tells us something the ancient
traditions cannot,

64
00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:32.140
not because it is wiser than they are,

65
00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:34.340
but because the machinery has not yet been

66
00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:37.220
hidden behind a curtain of scripture and
time.

67
00:03:38.873 --> 00:03:40.700
So this part is a close-up.

68
00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:46.040
One dharma, in a few hundred square kilometres
of teak forest, held up to the light.

69
00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:51.200
An illiterate farmer in the teak forests

70
00:03:53.474 --> 00:03:56.380
The man on the road has a name, or rather
several.

71
00:03:56.773 --> 00:04:00.360
He was born Raden Kohar, probably in 1859,

72
00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:05.220
in the Blora region of central Java: a
flat, hot country famous for its teak.

73
00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:09.340
As a grown man he took the name Samin
Surosentiko,

74
00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:12.280
and it is as "Samin" that he entered
history,

75
00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:15.840
mostly through the alarmed reports of the
people trying to govern him.

76
00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:21.580
There is a story about his father, and
even if it is only half true,

77
00:04:21.580 --> 00:04:24.980
it tells you something about where the
movement's spirit came from.

78
00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:30.620
By tradition, his father, remembered as
Samin Sepuh ("Samin the Elder"),

79
00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:32.500
was a nobleman from Ponorogo

80
00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:35.920
who renounced his aristocratic rank to
live as a commoner,

81
00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:38.320
in deliberate opposition to Dutch rule.

82
00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:40.840
Whether or not the bloodline is exact,

83
00:04:40.940 --> 00:04:43.340
the gesture is the seed of the whole
movement:

84
00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:47.440
a turning away from status, a choice to go
down rather than up.

85
00:04:49.144 --> 00:04:52.980
From about 1890, Samin began teaching in
his home village

86
00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:54.600
and the surrounding villages.

87
00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:57.720
What he taught was not, at first, a
revolt.

88
00:04:58.183 --> 00:04:59.660
It was a way of living.

89
00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:03.560
But it landed in a world that was being
squeezed hard,

90
00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:06.540
and that is what turned a quiet ethic into
a movement.

91
00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:14.160
In 1897 the Dutch colonial forestry
service drew a line around the teak forests

92
00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:17.420
that the villagers had always used -- for
firewood,

93
00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:22.040
for building, for grazing, for the thousand
small needs of a subsistence life --

94
00:05:22.220 --> 00:05:25.820
and declared them restricted: state
forest, off limits.

95
00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:31.000
At the same time, the colonial taxes
pressed down: a land rent,

96
00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:34.560
a poll tax, a tax on what little the
farmers produced.

97
00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:37.880
To a family living at the edge of enough,

98
00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:40.980
the fencing of the forest and the demand
for cash were hunger,

99
00:05:41.060 --> 00:05:44.080
made by a distant authority that gave
nothing back.

100
00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:48.720
This is the soil in which the Samin dharma
grew,

101
00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:51.660
and the pattern already has a name that
fits perfectly,

102
00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:55.860
from the anthropologist James C Scott: the
moral economy of the peasant.

103
00:05:56.760 --> 00:06:01.500
Scott argued that peasant communities carry
a deep value-system "irrevocably linked

104
00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:05.860
to subsistence requirements," a shared
sense of what people owe each other,

105
00:06:05.860 --> 00:06:10.660
and what a community is owed, organised
entirely around the basic right to survive.

106
00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:14.740
When an outside power, a market or a
colonial state,

107
00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:18.560
tears up those customary rights of access
in the name of profit or order,

108
00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:20.100
it takes more than resources.

109
00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:22.840
It violates a moral world.

110
00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:28.320
The Samin response was the defence of that
moral world, raised into a creed.

111
00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:31.740
And that creed spread.

112
00:06:32.380 --> 00:06:36.300
From Blora it travelled across an arc of
districts (Rembang,

113
00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:39.860
Pati, Kudus, Grobogan, Bojonegoro),

114
00:06:39.860 --> 00:06:41.920
reaching, by one estimate,

115
00:06:41.980 --> 00:06:46.100
somewhere between three and five thousand
followers by 1907.

116
00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:49.560
That was the year it became too visible to
ignore.

117
00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:53.000
The Dutch arrested Samin and exiled him to
Sawahlunto,

118
00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:58.540
in West Sumatra, far across the sea, where
he died in 1914.

119
00:06:58.940 --> 00:07:01.840
His followers did not accept that he had
simply died.

120
00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:05.240
They held that he had attained moksha
(release,

121
00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:10.680
liberation) and passed into the spiritual
realm, a detail that will matter later.

122
00:07:13.755 --> 00:07:14.460
The code

123
00:07:16.750 --> 00:07:19.080
So what did the Samin actually believe?

124
00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:22.920
What follows is a reconstruction through
other people's windows --

125
00:07:23.240 --> 00:07:26.100
I will make a fuller accounting of those
windows shortly --

126
00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:28.640
but its shape is consistent across them,

127
00:07:28.820 --> 00:07:31.820
and for a movement the Dutch treated as a
dangerous sect,

128
00:07:31.960 --> 00:07:34.400
the content is strikingly down-to-earth.

129
00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:37.660
There is barely any theology in it at all;

130
00:07:37.700 --> 00:07:40.600
it is a code for conduct, not a map of
heaven.

131
00:07:42.287 --> 00:07:46.540
The recurring principles, as best we can
reconstruct them, are these.

132
00:07:47.060 --> 00:07:49.560
Be honest, jujur, and speak plainly.

133
00:07:50.120 --> 00:07:52.800
Do no violence, and be self-sufficient:

134
00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:56.720
live by the work of your own hands, your
own farm.

135
00:07:57.300 --> 00:08:00.380
Keep life simple; do not chase material
excess.

136
00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:05.360
Do not disturb or harm other people, and
avoid envy, greed, and quarrelling.

137
00:08:05.980 --> 00:08:09.800
And do not take ill-gotten gain,
especially in trade and commerce:

138
00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:12.120
the Samin were deeply suspicious of the

139
00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:14.400
merchant's habit of getting something for
nothing.

140
00:08:16.260 --> 00:08:20.160
Underneath these everyday rules sits one
conviction with real teeth,

141
00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:21.720
the one that made the movement dangerous:

142
00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:27.600
that land, water, and forest are common
property, to be used for the common good.

143
00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:32.080
Not the king's, not the colonial state's,
not a company's.

144
00:08:32.780 --> 00:08:33.720
Everyone's.

145
00:08:34.600 --> 00:08:37.320
That single idea is the doctrinal root of
both the

146
00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:40.760
tax-and-forest resistance of Samin's own
day and,

147
00:08:40.760 --> 00:08:44.780
as we will see, the environmental struggle
his descendants are still fighting now.

148
00:08:46.588 --> 00:08:49.860
They called their faith Agama Adam, "the
religion of Adam."

149
00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:53.540
The name is a claim: that this was
humanity's original religion,

150
00:08:53.660 --> 00:08:57.320
the simple thing we all had before the
priests and the empires got to us.

151
00:08:57.700 --> 00:09:01.600
Its practice was correspondingly bare:
prayer at dawn and dusk,

152
00:09:01.640 --> 00:09:04.200
and almost none of the elaborate
ceremonial apparatus

153
00:09:04.280 --> 00:09:06.420
that surrounded Javanese village life.

154
00:09:06.860 --> 00:09:11.800
No grand rituals, no professional clergy,
just a plain ethic for plain people.

155
00:09:14.021 --> 00:09:15.900
The whole thing was carried by mouth.

156
00:09:16.460 --> 00:09:19.100
Samin was illiterate, and so were his
followers,

157
00:09:19.180 --> 00:09:23.260
so there was no scripture, no holy book to
copy, guard, and argue over.

158
00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:28.300
The ethic travelled by recitation, by
example, and by embodied practice,

159
00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:31.380
including a discipline called laku jejek,

160
00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:34.120
days of walking to reach a community
gathering.

161
00:09:34.460 --> 00:09:38.800
A dharma with no text, held in nothing but
memory and the soles of people's feet.

162
00:09:40.660 --> 00:09:44.840
That picture needs one qualification, and
the exception is revealing.

163
00:09:45.420 --> 00:09:47.400
A handful of texts are, in fact,

164
00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:49.780
attributed to the tradition (most often a

165
00:09:49.780 --> 00:09:52.780
manuscript called the Serat Jamus
Kalimosodo),

166
00:09:52.780 --> 00:09:57.580
and some Samin families keep a kris, the
wavy ceremonial blade of Java,

167
00:09:57.600 --> 00:10:00.940
as an heirloom that carries the teaching
down the generations.

168
00:10:01.420 --> 00:10:03.620
So the Samin were not quite textless;

169
00:10:03.620 --> 00:10:07.500
they were deeply suspicious of the written
word as the instrument of the mullah,

170
00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:09.300
the clerk, and the tax-collector,

171
00:10:09.300 --> 00:10:12.920
and inclined to trust instead the things a
person keeps in the body.

172
00:10:14.660 --> 00:10:17.860
Javanese spiritual life has a word for
this: laku,

173
00:10:17.960 --> 00:10:19.960
meaning something close to "conduct,"

174
00:10:20.060 --> 00:10:25.100
or "the path you walk," a discipline you
practise rather than a doctrine you read.

175
00:10:25.620 --> 00:10:29.560
Seen that way, the laku jejek, those days
of walking to a gathering,

176
00:10:29.560 --> 00:10:31.920
was not a quaint custom bolted onto the
ethic;

177
00:10:32.140 --> 00:10:34.460
it was the ethic, rehearsed in the legs.

178
00:10:34.980 --> 00:10:39.060
The deadpan literalism on the forest road
belonged to the same family:

179
00:10:39.300 --> 00:10:41.060
honesty drilled into a reflex,

180
00:10:41.260 --> 00:10:45.720
plain speech worn so smooth it could
answer an empire without stopping to think.

181
00:10:46.120 --> 00:10:48.020
How a dharma installs itself,

182
00:10:48.140 --> 00:10:52.000
through what its people repeatedly do at
least as much as through what they believe,

183
00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:54.720
is a story for a later part; the Samin,

184
00:10:54.720 --> 00:10:58.080
with no scripture to argue over, had
almost nothing but the doing.

185
00:10:59.020 --> 00:11:02.880
It made them very hard to convert and even
harder to stamp out.

186
00:11:03.340 --> 00:11:05.580
You cannot burn a book that was never
written;

187
00:11:05.580 --> 00:11:08.460
you can only try to outlast the people who
walk it,

188
00:11:08.680 --> 00:11:12.300
and on that score the Samin have proved
stubbornly patient,

189
00:11:12.400 --> 00:11:14.400
generation after generation.

190
00:11:16.140 --> 00:11:19.240
One more thing, before we go on, about
names.

191
00:11:19.660 --> 00:11:23.820
The followers themselves came to dislike
the word "Samin" because,

192
00:11:23.820 --> 00:11:27.360
in mainstream Indonesian society, it
picked up a sneer:

193
00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:30.260
a synonym for backward, foolish,
primitive.

194
00:11:31.380 --> 00:11:34.320
They prefer to call themselves Sedulur
Sikep,

195
00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:38.260
or wong sikep: roughly, "honest,
responsible kin";

196
00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:40.480
a wong sikep, in the older idiom,

197
00:11:40.480 --> 00:11:44.580
is "a man who embraces," one who embraces
his wife and his land.

198
00:11:45.480 --> 00:11:48.640
It is a better name, and from here I will
use both:

199
00:11:48.920 --> 00:11:52.620
theirs by preference, and "Samin" where
the history insists.

200
00:11:55.852 --> 00:11:57.040
But is it a dharma?

201
00:11:59.376 --> 00:12:01.640
Now, a sceptic could stop me right here.

202
00:12:02.240 --> 00:12:05.240
Plenty of villages have customs, the
sceptic says.

203
00:12:05.840 --> 00:12:10.160
Lots of communities are truthful, simple,
and suspicious of outsiders.

204
00:12:10.740 --> 00:12:14.020
You have described a set of folkways with
a local accent.

205
00:12:14.600 --> 00:12:17.020
Why dignify it with a word like "dharma"?

206
00:12:17.640 --> 00:12:20.360
Aren't you just doing the thing this
project warned against,

207
00:12:20.700 --> 00:12:23.460
slapping an exotic label on ordinary
peasant life?

208
00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:26.680
It is a fair challenge,

209
00:12:26.680 --> 00:12:30.140
and the Samin are where our definition
gets tested against a real case.

210
00:12:30.840 --> 00:12:33.760
Back in Part 1, we worked out what
separates a dharma

211
00:12:33.760 --> 00:12:35.860
from mere custom or law or etiquette.

212
00:12:36.560 --> 00:12:39.320
Not its content (there is no single belief

213
00:12:39.320 --> 00:12:42.960
that makes something a dharma) but a
combination of features.

214
00:12:43.507 --> 00:12:47.760
I will run the Sedulur Sikep against that
test, in plain language.

215
00:12:49.599 --> 00:12:51.720
First: is it comprehensive?

216
00:12:52.080 --> 00:12:55.700
Does it govern a whole life, or just one
corner of behaviour?

217
00:12:56.140 --> 00:12:58.460
The Sikep code clearly does the former.

218
00:12:58.920 --> 00:13:02.700
It tells you how to work, how to speak,
what you may own,

219
00:13:02.700 --> 00:13:04.060
how to treat your neighbours,

220
00:13:04.080 --> 00:13:07.040
how to deal with authority, even how to
understand death.

221
00:13:08.060 --> 00:13:11.840
It is an architecture for living, not a
rule about one thing.

222
00:13:13.684 --> 00:13:18.660
Second: is it identity-constituting, does
it tell people who they are?

223
00:13:19.240 --> 00:13:20.680
Unmistakably.

224
00:13:21.160 --> 00:13:24.940
To be Sedulur Sikep is to be one of "the
honest people."

225
00:13:25.180 --> 00:13:28.960
It is a self; you can hear it in the very
name they chose.

226
00:13:30.944 --> 00:13:36.120
Third: is it oriented toward the good,
rather than toward mere advantage?

227
00:13:36.820 --> 00:13:37.520
Yes.

228
00:13:38.160 --> 00:13:41.200
The wong sikep were after a particular
kind of life,

229
00:13:41.340 --> 00:13:44.280
not the most profitable one -- autonomous,

230
00:13:44.300 --> 00:13:47.400
truthful, harmonious, rooted in the land
--

231
00:13:47.720 --> 00:13:51.080
and they paid for it in taxes refused and
forests lost.

232
00:13:53.360 --> 00:13:57.840
And the fourth feature is the decisive
one: is it reflective and self-aware?

233
00:13:58.460 --> 00:14:02.260
Here is where this dharma and mere custom
part company for good.

234
00:14:02.760 --> 00:14:07.840
Ordinary village tradition is usually
inherited and seldom held up for examination.

235
00:14:08.340 --> 00:14:11.360
You do it because it is done, because your
parents did it,

236
00:14:11.360 --> 00:14:13.280
because that is simply how things are.

237
00:14:13.740 --> 00:14:18.100
What the Sedulur Sikep did is far rarer,
and they did it out loud.

238
00:14:18.690 --> 00:14:23.300
They consciously chose a set of values,
lifted them out of the surrounding culture,

239
00:14:23.480 --> 00:14:26.380
gave them a name, and defended them
deliberately,

240
00:14:26.420 --> 00:14:28.660
against the dominant order of their day.

241
00:14:29.760 --> 00:14:32.720
They knew they were being different, and
they meant to be.

242
00:14:34.560 --> 00:14:36.940
That deliberate lifting is precisely what
turned

243
00:14:36.940 --> 00:14:39.460
the Sedulur Sikep's folkways into a
dharma.

244
00:14:40.060 --> 00:14:44.240
And it is why the Samin pass the test
where mere custom would fail it.

245
00:14:44.836 --> 00:14:47.820
They were practising a chosen way, and
they knew it,

246
00:14:47.820 --> 00:14:51.320
and they would tell you so, in blunt
language, to your face.

247
00:14:53.220 --> 00:14:54.900
Which brings us to the language.

248
00:14:58.080 --> 00:14:59.520
Speaking ngoko to the king

249
00:15:02.258 --> 00:15:05.180
To feel how radical the wong sikep really
were,

250
00:15:05.280 --> 00:15:09.380
you have to see what they were rebelling
against, and it was not only the Dutch.

251
00:15:10.040 --> 00:15:14.020
It was the entire intricate hierarchy of
Javanese culture itself.

252
00:15:15.986 --> 00:15:19.920
The classic map of that culture comes from
anthropologist Clifford Geertz,

253
00:15:19.920 --> 00:15:25.180
who in 1960 divided Javanese society into
three streams: the abangan,

254
00:15:25.180 --> 00:15:27.760
the syncretic peasant majority who blended
Islam

255
00:15:27.760 --> 00:15:31.180
with older mystical and animist
traditions; the santri,

256
00:15:31.180 --> 00:15:33.780
the devout, orthodox Muslims; and the
priyayi,

257
00:15:34.240 --> 00:15:37.600
the refined aristocratic-bureaucratic
class tied to the courts.

258
00:15:38.540 --> 00:15:42.820
Scholars have argued about this scheme
ever since (it is an ideal-type,

259
00:15:42.820 --> 00:15:46.460
not a perfect description), but it gives
us the lay of the land.

260
00:15:48.340 --> 00:15:51.800
And running through all of it was an
exquisitely graded etiquette,

261
00:15:51.860 --> 00:15:54.260
encoded in the Javanese language itself.

262
00:15:55.000 --> 00:15:56.420
Javanese has levels.

263
00:15:57.120 --> 00:16:01.440
There is ngoko, blunt and low, the speech
you use with intimates and inferiors;

264
00:16:01.640 --> 00:16:03.880
there is krama, high and refined,

265
00:16:03.880 --> 00:16:06.740
the speech you use to show respect to your
superiors;

266
00:16:06.740 --> 00:16:08.860
and there is a middle register between
them.

267
00:16:09.140 --> 00:16:11.480
In ordinary life, you perform your place
in

268
00:16:11.480 --> 00:16:13.720
the social order every time you open your
mouth.

269
00:16:14.453 --> 00:16:17.080
You address the person above you in
deferential krama,

270
00:16:17.080 --> 00:16:18.880
and you receive ngoko in return,

271
00:16:18.880 --> 00:16:23.160
and the whole hierarchy is rehearsed,
sentence by sentence, all day long.

272
00:16:24.972 --> 00:16:27.480
The Sedulur Sikep refused the entire
arrangement.

273
00:16:28.692 --> 00:16:34.560
They spoke plain, low ngoko to everyone:
to each other, to officials, to anyone.

274
00:16:35.220 --> 00:16:37.280
To address a colonial administrator

275
00:16:37.380 --> 00:16:41.180
or a Javanese aristocrat in the same blunt
register you would use

276
00:16:41.180 --> 00:16:43.880
with a child or a friend was a deliberate
linguistic

277
00:16:43.920 --> 00:16:46.540
refusal of the whole hierarchy of
deference.

278
00:16:47.240 --> 00:16:51.440
With grammar alone, they declined to admit
that anyone stood above them.

279
00:16:53.230 --> 00:16:54.400
They went further.

280
00:16:55.140 --> 00:16:59.660
Mainstream Javanese culture prized the
ideal of halus (the refined,

281
00:16:59.660 --> 00:17:01.900
indirect, emotionally controlled,

282
00:17:01.900 --> 00:17:03.700
and exquisitely polite person)

283
00:17:03.900 --> 00:17:08.040
and looked down on kasar (the coarse,
blunt, and direct).

284
00:17:08.740 --> 00:17:12.720
The wong sikep took that prestige ranking
and turned it on its head.

285
00:17:13.300 --> 00:17:17.900
They made plain, literal, sometimes
maddening truth-telling into a virtue.

286
00:17:18.540 --> 00:17:21.740
The deadpan answers on the forest road
were not stupidity.

287
00:17:22.740 --> 00:17:24.700
They were halus turned inside out:

288
00:17:24.860 --> 00:17:27.880
a refusal to play the game of refined
indirection,

289
00:17:27.900 --> 00:17:31.060
weaponised into politeness so literal it
became a wall.

290
00:17:33.080 --> 00:17:36.100
They left the religion box blank on their
identity cards.

291
00:17:36.640 --> 00:17:41.580
They minimised the ceremonial life, the
elaborate ritual meals and observances,

292
00:17:41.720 --> 00:17:44.720
that gave mainstream Javanese religion its
texture.

293
00:17:45.220 --> 00:17:48.180
And, following old Samin Sepuh's example,

294
00:17:48.180 --> 00:17:51.360
they refused the whole
aristocratic-bureaucratic order:

295
00:17:51.400 --> 00:17:56.180
no tax, no forced labour, no marriage
registration, no census.

296
00:17:58.060 --> 00:17:59.920
But this creed was Java's own.

297
00:18:00.220 --> 00:18:03.640
The Sedulur Sikep were agrarian, mystical,

298
00:18:03.660 --> 00:18:06.760
and rooted in the very same peasant world
they were reforming;

299
00:18:07.020 --> 00:18:09.640
their suspicion of orthodox Islam was
itself

300
00:18:09.640 --> 00:18:12.160
steeped in heterodox Javanese mysticism.

301
00:18:12.800 --> 00:18:16.940
They took Java's own materials and used
them against Java's own hierarchy.

302
00:18:17.640 --> 00:18:21.800
The best name for that is not rebellion
from without but heresy from within:

303
00:18:21.880 --> 00:18:26.220
an internal reform that knows the tradition
intimately, because it is made of it.

304
00:18:28.010 --> 00:18:32.240
A dharma, in other words, can be grown at
home, out of the same soil,

305
00:18:32.240 --> 00:18:34.940
in deliberate argument with the culture
that surrounds it.

306
00:18:37.860 --> 00:18:39.980
"From the forest, to the forest"

307
00:18:42.160 --> 00:18:45.140
Return, for a moment, to the deadpan on
the road.

308
00:18:45.560 --> 00:18:47.980
It would be easy to file it under
eccentricity.

309
00:18:48.560 --> 00:18:50.740
It was the Sikep dharma in action.

310
00:18:52.460 --> 00:18:55.800
James C Scott, the same scholar who gave
us the moral economy,

311
00:18:55.800 --> 00:18:59.360
spent much of his career studying how
powerless people resist powerful ones

312
00:18:59.360 --> 00:19:00.480
without open battle,

313
00:19:00.480 --> 00:19:01.780
which they cannot win.

314
00:19:02.200 --> 00:19:05.380
He called the result the weapons of the
weak: foot-dragging,

315
00:19:05.460 --> 00:19:09.220
feigned ignorance, false compliance,
deliberate misunderstanding,

316
00:19:09.280 --> 00:19:12.760
the slow quiet sabotage of people who
cannot say no out loud

317
00:19:12.760 --> 00:19:15.060
and so say it in a thousand other ways.

318
00:19:15.690 --> 00:19:18.420
He called the hidden world of grumbling
and mockery,

319
00:19:18.420 --> 00:19:20.680
the one the powerful never get to see,

320
00:19:20.680 --> 00:19:24.080
the hidden transcript, and the whole
domain of this everyday,

321
00:19:24.080 --> 00:19:26.480
below-the-radar resistance infra-politics.

322
00:19:27.380 --> 00:19:31.940
(Scott never studied the Samin himself;
but his frame describes them exactly.)

323
00:19:33.320 --> 00:19:37.100
Read through Scott, the wong sikep's
tactics snap into focus.

324
00:19:37.600 --> 00:19:39.540
Answering an official with perfect,

325
00:19:39.680 --> 00:19:43.160
useless literalism is feigned ignorance
raised to an art.

326
00:19:43.740 --> 00:19:47.180
Refusing to register a marriage, declining
the census,

327
00:19:47.300 --> 00:19:49.400
paying only what cannot be avoided:

328
00:19:49.500 --> 00:19:52.380
these are the weapons of the weak,
deployed with discipline.

329
00:19:53.200 --> 00:19:57.240
What makes the Sedulur Sikep extraordinary
is that they took this scattered,

330
00:19:57.240 --> 00:20:01.040
instinctive repertoire of peasant
resistance and moralised it,

331
00:20:01.100 --> 00:20:05.120
turning it into a named, principled,
identity-bearing way of life.

332
00:20:05.720 --> 00:20:10.040
Most peasants drag their feet and keep the
reasons in the hidden transcript.

333
00:20:10.580 --> 00:20:14.140
The wong sikep dragged their feet on
principle, and were proud.

334
00:20:14.780 --> 00:20:17.400
Ethics and tactics had fused into one
thing.

335
00:20:19.400 --> 00:20:24.040
And beneath the tactics, it turns out, lay
something like a philosophy of language.

336
00:20:24.320 --> 00:20:28.360
In the recorded testimony of one follower
arrested in 1928,

337
00:20:28.360 --> 00:20:30.160
to which we will come shortly,

338
00:20:30.160 --> 00:20:32.800
the words that truly mattered were the few
about work,

339
00:20:32.800 --> 00:20:34.500
marriage, and upright conduct;

340
00:20:34.680 --> 00:20:39.460
nearly every other word could be hollowed
out or repurposed, often by punning,

341
00:20:39.500 --> 00:20:43.340
so that "religion" could shade into the
word for a weapon and "tax" could,

342
00:20:43.340 --> 00:20:45.540
in the right mouth, mean nothing at all.

343
00:20:46.020 --> 00:20:49.600
A word, in this thinking, means what its
utterer means by it,

344
00:20:49.600 --> 00:20:53.840
and an empire's word, spoken by an empire,
could be handed back empty.

345
00:20:54.380 --> 00:20:59.000
The deadpan on the forest road was not
merely feigned ignorance raised to an art.

346
00:20:59.300 --> 00:21:03.580
It was doctrine: a quiet claim of
sovereignty over meaning itself.

347
00:21:05.490 --> 00:21:08.940
And a deeper current runs underneath, one
we have already met.

348
00:21:09.460 --> 00:21:13.280
Christopher Boehm's reverse dominance
hierarchy, from Part 2,

349
00:21:13.280 --> 00:21:16.340
was his account of how human morality may
have begun:

350
00:21:16.520 --> 00:21:20.820
as a device against domination, the many
using ridicule, shaming,

351
00:21:20.820 --> 00:21:24.900
and ostracism to cut down anyone who tried
to lord it over the group.

352
00:21:25.300 --> 00:21:28.820
If he is even partly right, then a dharma
like the Samin's,

353
00:21:28.900 --> 00:21:32.620
defined almost entirely by its refusal of
a domineering state,

354
00:21:32.620 --> 00:21:35.680
is morality doing one of the oldest jobs
it ever had.

355
00:21:36.020 --> 00:21:39.700
The man on the forest road, answering the
empire with riddles,

356
00:21:39.720 --> 00:21:41.700
was standing in a very long line.

357
00:21:45.330 --> 00:21:46.080
A pause

358
00:21:48.740 --> 00:21:53.460
I have been building a case, and before I
finish it, three warnings are in order,

359
00:21:53.460 --> 00:21:57.620
because a dharma is exactly the kind of
thing it is easy to romanticise,

360
00:21:57.620 --> 00:22:00.260
and I have been as charmed by this one as
anyone.

361
00:22:02.500 --> 00:22:04.140
The first is about the evidence.

362
00:22:04.680 --> 00:22:08.780
Almost everything we "know" about Samin
doctrine comes to us second-hand,

363
00:22:08.780 --> 00:22:11.220
and often third-hand, from outsiders:

364
00:22:11.380 --> 00:22:14.660
Dutch colonial officials and, later,
ethnographers.

365
00:22:15.240 --> 00:22:18.500
The reason is the very thing that makes
the movement so striking:

366
00:22:18.500 --> 00:22:20.420
the Samin wrote nothing down.

367
00:22:20.960 --> 00:22:26.060
The historians Harry Benda and Lance Castles,
in their foundational scholarly study,

368
00:22:26.060 --> 00:22:28.440
make this their central caution and note

369
00:22:28.440 --> 00:22:31.660
that the accounts we do have were
frequently written by hostile

370
00:22:31.660 --> 00:22:33.360
or baffled observers.

371
00:22:33.780 --> 00:22:35.760
We are watching this dharma through a
series

372
00:22:35.760 --> 00:22:38.580
of other people's windows, some of them
grimy.

373
00:22:40.140 --> 00:22:42.960
There is one window that is less grimy
than the rest,

374
00:22:43.040 --> 00:22:46.240
and what can be seen through it should
adjust the whole picture.

375
00:22:46.500 --> 00:22:49.400
The historian Takashi Shiraishi dug out of
the colonial

376
00:22:49.400 --> 00:22:52.540
archive the minutes of the interrogation
of Dangir,

377
00:22:52.580 --> 00:22:55.920
a twenty-five-year-old follower arrested
in 1928,

378
00:22:56.000 --> 00:22:59.340
who answered a native official's questions
about his faith at length

379
00:22:59.340 --> 00:23:00.640
and in his own idiom:

380
00:23:00.640 --> 00:23:03.820
how he was initiated with five aphorisms
about walking;

381
00:23:04.220 --> 00:23:06.580
why the ritual meal that sealed it could
be paid

382
00:23:06.580 --> 00:23:08.860
for only with money from his own farm
labour,

383
00:23:08.860 --> 00:23:12.520
never from trade, "because traders usually
get profit by cheating";

384
00:23:12.740 --> 00:23:15.200
what an honest man may and may not do.

385
00:23:16.360 --> 00:23:20.440
It is the nearest thing we have to a Samin
speaking, and even it reaches us,

386
00:23:20.440 --> 00:23:23.000
as Shiraishi says, doubly alienated,

387
00:23:23.140 --> 00:23:25.240
filtered through the official who wrote it
down.

388
00:23:26.080 --> 00:23:28.960
But a voice it is, and it says something
startling:

389
00:23:29.080 --> 00:23:31.200
Dangir denied being a "Samin" at all.

390
00:23:31.720 --> 00:23:35.160
The word meant nothing to him; it was the
government's label.

391
00:23:35.560 --> 00:23:38.420
He called himself wong sikep, a man who
embraces,

392
00:23:38.680 --> 00:23:40.440
living the religion of Adam he had learned

393
00:23:40.440 --> 00:23:42.360
from a neighbouring teacher he could name.

394
00:23:44.300 --> 00:23:45.500
Shiraishi's larger,

395
00:23:45.500 --> 00:23:46.760
uncomfortable lesson is

396
00:23:47.000 --> 00:23:49.400
that some of what the record calls "the
Samin

397
00:23:49.400 --> 00:23:52.420
movement" is an artefact of the record
itself:

398
00:23:52.680 --> 00:23:56.000
colonial clerks joining scattered circles
of quiet people

399
00:23:56.000 --> 00:23:57.940
into one remembered conspiracy,

400
00:23:58.100 --> 00:24:00.640
and some of the wilder material in the
files,

401
00:24:00.740 --> 00:24:04.100
the prophecies of coming kings that fed
the millenarian reading,

402
00:24:04.180 --> 00:24:06.820
arriving by way of paid informers telling
their

403
00:24:06.820 --> 00:24:09.400
masters what subversion ought to sound
like.

404
00:24:10.200 --> 00:24:13.000
Even the scholars disagree about what they
are looking at:

405
00:24:13.140 --> 00:24:16.480
some read the Samin as an
ethical-religious resistance movement,

406
00:24:16.800 --> 00:24:21.060
some as a millenarian one waiting for
salvation and the return of their founder,

407
00:24:21.060 --> 00:24:24.900
some as hard-headed peasants rationally
defending their livelihoods;

408
00:24:25.180 --> 00:24:26.580
and after Shiraishi,

409
00:24:26.580 --> 00:24:30.300
the second reading stands on visibly
shakier ground than the others.

410
00:24:30.920 --> 00:24:35.100
When I tell you what the Samin "believed,"
please hear the quotation marks.

411
00:24:35.860 --> 00:24:40.380
A dharma this good deserves a plain
accounting of how little we can be sure of.

412
00:24:42.180 --> 00:24:45.120
The second is sharper, and we have to face
it squarely.

413
00:24:46.070 --> 00:24:48.140
The wong sikep were not secular.

414
00:24:48.590 --> 00:24:52.300
I have spent so long discussing them
because they look, at first,

415
00:24:52.300 --> 00:24:54.840
like a do-it-yourself ethic with no god in
it,

416
00:24:54.940 --> 00:24:58.460
a practical code about fair dealing,
forests, and taxes.

417
00:24:59.480 --> 00:25:01.140
But that is not the whole truth.

418
00:25:01.640 --> 00:25:06.540
They had Agama Adam, the religion of Adam;
they believed in an eternal soul,

419
00:25:06.560 --> 00:25:09.520
and that their founder did not die but
attained moksha.

420
00:25:10.120 --> 00:25:14.840
There is metaphysics here, and a cosmology,
and a hope that reaches past this life.

421
00:25:16.560 --> 00:25:19.480
So why are they here at all when, in the
end,

422
00:25:19.480 --> 00:25:23.180
the question is whether we can build
ethical worlds without the supernatural?

423
00:25:25.040 --> 00:25:26.780
Because they are a boundary case,

424
00:25:26.900 --> 00:25:30.100
and boundary cases are worth more than
tidy examples.

425
00:25:30.720 --> 00:25:35.740
The Sedulur Sikep are not a world religion,
with scripture, clergy, and empire.

426
00:25:36.350 --> 00:25:41.100
But they are not a secular philosophy
either, like Stoicism stripped of its gods.

427
00:25:41.840 --> 00:25:46.700
They sit in between: a non-scriptural,
non-institutional, this-worldly,

428
00:25:46.700 --> 00:25:48.380
ethics-first folk dharma,

429
00:25:48.400 --> 00:25:52.220
with just enough cosmology to give it a
backbone and not an ounce more.

430
00:25:52.780 --> 00:25:56.000
There is a whole middle country of ways of
living that are neither,

431
00:25:56.020 --> 00:25:59.060
and the Sedulur Sikep are its first sure
landmark.

432
00:25:59.740 --> 00:26:02.320
For an argument that dharmas come in every
form,

433
00:26:02.380 --> 00:26:06.240
an example that refuses our neat boxes is
not an embarrassment.

434
00:26:06.960 --> 00:26:08.940
It is the best evidence we have.

435
00:26:11.040 --> 00:26:13.980
The third warning I can only post here and
honour later,

436
00:26:14.160 --> 00:26:16.860
because it needs a standard this book has
not yet built.

437
00:26:17.260 --> 00:26:21.660
A dharma this admirable still had hard
edges, and they pressed on its own people:

438
00:26:21.840 --> 00:26:25.780
for most of a century the wong sikep kept
their children from school,

439
00:26:25.780 --> 00:26:29.320
and with it from letters, rather than hand
them to the state's classroom;

440
00:26:29.940 --> 00:26:30.900
and the same tradition

441
00:26:30.900 --> 00:26:35.300
that will shortly give us nine heroic
women at a palace gate also prescribed --

442
00:26:35.500 --> 00:26:39.520
and in the one village fieldworkers have
studied closely still prescribes --

443
00:26:39.600 --> 00:26:42.780
a wife's place in a plainly patriarchal
household.

444
00:26:43.580 --> 00:26:45.520
Whether a way of life can hold this well

445
00:26:45.520 --> 00:26:48.540
without crushing anyone inside it is a
real question,

446
00:26:48.560 --> 00:26:52.940
not a rhetorical one, and Part 8 will put
this very dharma through that test,

447
00:26:53.160 --> 00:26:55.640
without flinching, as its hardest case.

448
00:26:58.760 --> 00:26:59.620
Feet in cement

449
00:27:01.760 --> 00:27:05.040
The strongest proof that the wong sikep
built a real dharma,

450
00:27:05.040 --> 00:27:09.260
and not a one-generation protest, is that
it did not die with Samin.

451
00:27:09.540 --> 00:27:11.040
It is still being lived,

452
00:27:11.140 --> 00:27:13.020
and it has crossed a hundred years

453
00:27:13.100 --> 00:27:16.460
and an entire change of subject without
losing its spine.

454
00:27:18.240 --> 00:27:22.060
The century in between was not quiet, and
it is worth pausing on,

455
00:27:22.060 --> 00:27:24.980
because it shows what the refusal was
really aimed at.

456
00:27:25.320 --> 00:27:27.940
When the Dutch left, the refusal stayed,

457
00:27:27.940 --> 00:27:31.020
since the thing being refused had never
really been the Dutch;

458
00:27:31.040 --> 00:27:33.820
it was the state's claim to name and
number people.

459
00:27:34.280 --> 00:27:38.260
Anthropologist Amrih Widodo, who has
followed the community for decades,

460
00:27:38.300 --> 00:27:41.240
records what that meant under Suharto's
New Order:

461
00:27:41.520 --> 00:27:44.440
pressure to fill in the religion column on
the identity card,

462
00:27:44.440 --> 00:27:47.200
since the religion of Adam was not on the
official list;

463
00:27:47.460 --> 00:27:51.460
mass wedding ceremonies arranged to pull
unregistered Sikep marriages

464
00:27:51.660 --> 00:27:53.780
into the books of the religious
bureaucracy;

465
00:27:54.100 --> 00:27:58.980
and, for those who declined, the threat of
transmigration off Java altogether.

466
00:27:59.160 --> 00:28:02.440
The flags had changed and the census came
anyway,

467
00:28:02.440 --> 00:28:05.480
and the Sedulur Sikep answered the
independent republic much

468
00:28:05.580 --> 00:28:07.380
as they had answered the empire.

469
00:28:09.120 --> 00:28:11.160
Travel to the Kendeng mountains today,

470
00:28:11.300 --> 00:28:13.960
and you will find the Sedulur Sikep still
there,

471
00:28:13.960 --> 00:28:17.580
still honest, still stubborn, and now
fighting a new enemy.

472
00:28:17.960 --> 00:28:20.160
The Kendeng is karst country: soft,

473
00:28:20.800 --> 00:28:23.940
porous limestone that soaks up rain and
feeds the springs

474
00:28:23.940 --> 00:28:26.240
and rivers the farmers below depend on.

475
00:28:26.620 --> 00:28:29.600
Since the 2000s, they have been at the
forefront of

476
00:28:29.600 --> 00:28:32.920
non-violent resistance across the
districts of Rembang, Pati,

477
00:28:33.460 --> 00:28:36.040
and Grobogan against limestone quarrying

478
00:28:36.040 --> 00:28:40.520
and a large cement plant operated by the
company PT Semen Indonesia.

479
00:28:41.080 --> 00:28:44.700
Their argument is the very same one their
great-grandparents made

480
00:28:44.700 --> 00:28:45.840
to the Dutch foresters:

481
00:28:46.040 --> 00:28:49.360
that land, water, and forest are common
property,

482
00:28:49.360 --> 00:28:53.060
and that protecting the karst, the rock
that holds the region's water,

483
00:28:53.060 --> 00:28:56.380
is a sacred duty, not a thing to be
blasted away for profit.

484
00:28:58.380 --> 00:29:01.640
And in 2016, and again in 2017,

485
00:29:01.640 --> 00:29:04.960
they made that argument in the most
physical way imaginable:

486
00:29:05.020 --> 00:29:07.740
nine women at the gates of the
Presidential Palace,

487
00:29:07.740 --> 00:29:09.920
with wet cement poured around their feet.

488
00:29:10.820 --> 00:29:13.140
They are remembered as the Kartini
Kendeng,

489
00:29:13.200 --> 00:29:16.780
after Indonesia's pioneering heroine of
women's emancipation.

490
00:29:17.780 --> 00:29:22.200
The image is almost unbearably apt: the
wong cilik,

491
00:29:22.200 --> 00:29:26.600
the "little people," literally encased in
the very substance the industry wanted

492
00:29:26.600 --> 00:29:28.060
to make from their mountains,

493
00:29:28.280 --> 00:29:32.300
immobilised in the capital to dramatise
the immobilising of their land.

494
00:29:33.220 --> 00:29:35.320
They held there in the heat and the noise.

495
00:29:35.960 --> 00:29:38.980
Patmi did not long survive the second of
those protests:

496
00:29:39.120 --> 00:29:42.900
the night after the cement came off her
feet, her heart gave out.

497
00:29:44.540 --> 00:29:46.580
Why was it women who made that journey,

498
00:29:46.580 --> 00:29:49.300
and women whose names the protest still
carries?

499
00:29:49.660 --> 00:29:52.820
They named themselves after Raden Ajeng
Kartini,

500
00:29:52.820 --> 00:29:55.100
the Javanese noblewoman whose letters,

501
00:29:55.100 --> 00:29:57.040
written in Samin's own lifetime,

502
00:29:57.100 --> 00:30:00.540
became the founding text of Indonesian
women's emancipation.

503
00:30:00.780 --> 00:30:05.020
And they came not in spite of being
mothers and farmers but because of it,

504
00:30:05.200 --> 00:30:07.240
framing the defence of the water and the
soil

505
00:30:07.240 --> 00:30:11.720
as one continuous piece with the daily
work of keeping a family fed:

506
00:30:11.800 --> 00:30:14.840
the mountains were the kitchen, the
springs were the tap,

507
00:30:14.880 --> 00:30:17.940
and a cement company was proposing to
demolish both.

508
00:30:18.280 --> 00:30:21.360
A farmer named Sukinah made that argument
her own.

509
00:30:21.660 --> 00:30:23.980
There is a quiet brilliance in the tactic,

510
00:30:24.060 --> 00:30:26.840
because by standing on the most
traditional ground available

511
00:30:26.920 --> 00:30:29.300
to them (motherhood, the household,

512
00:30:29.300 --> 00:30:33.600
the duty to feed) these women advanced a
genuinely radical claim about

513
00:30:33.600 --> 00:30:35.060
who owns a landscape,

514
00:30:35.060 --> 00:30:39.440
and dared the state to be seen hauling
mothers in cement away from the palace gate.

515
00:30:39.880 --> 00:30:42.480
It is the old Samin move, intact.

516
00:30:44.040 --> 00:30:46.520
The law, for once, sided with the farmers:

517
00:30:46.840 --> 00:30:50.940
Indonesia's Supreme Court ruled in their
favour in October 2016.

518
00:30:51.320 --> 00:30:54.940
And then the provincial governor issued a
fresh permit within days,

519
00:30:55.000 --> 00:30:56.920
and the plant went ahead anyway:

520
00:30:56.920 --> 00:31:00.000
a bleak little lesson in the difference
between winning a ruling

521
00:31:00.100 --> 00:31:01.280
and winning a fight.

522
00:31:02.940 --> 00:31:05.520
But look at what has happened to the Sikep
dharma.

523
00:31:05.800 --> 00:31:08.580
The colonial-era creed -- common property,

524
00:31:08.580 --> 00:31:11.820
non-violence, refusal of an extractive
authority --

525
00:31:11.820 --> 00:31:15.280
has been recoded for an age its founder
could never have imagined.

526
00:31:15.840 --> 00:31:18.640
And the recoding was work, done in
company:

527
00:31:18.660 --> 00:31:23.060
the Sedulur Sikep stand today inside a
wider network of Kendeng farmers,

528
00:31:23.280 --> 00:31:27.240
joined by lawyers, environmental groups
and city sympathisers;

529
00:31:27.520 --> 00:31:31.740
they borrow the nation's own icons
(Kartini was no Samin);

530
00:31:31.820 --> 00:31:35.920
and they argue in courtrooms their
great-grandparents would have refused to enter.

531
00:31:36.360 --> 00:31:39.160
Continuity of spine is not sameness of
body.

532
00:31:39.520 --> 00:31:42.880
The enemy is no longer a Dutch forester
with a tax ledger;

533
00:31:43.140 --> 00:31:45.800
it is a cement company with a court-proof
permit.

534
00:31:46.800 --> 00:31:48.980
The forest has become the watershed.

535
00:31:49.860 --> 00:31:52.620
And the deadpan refusal on the road has
become a woman

536
00:31:52.620 --> 00:31:55.440
with her feet in concrete, refusing to
move.

537
00:31:56.500 --> 00:31:58.740
Same spine, new century.

538
00:31:59.040 --> 00:32:00.980
That is what a living dharma looks like:

539
00:32:00.980 --> 00:32:05.040
a way of living flexible enough to meet a
threat its makers never foresaw,

540
00:32:05.040 --> 00:32:09.060
and rigid enough, at the core, to still be
recognisably itself.

541
00:32:12.620 --> 00:32:13.980
What the wong sikep know

542
00:32:16.270 --> 00:32:19.900
So what was waiting, all along, in the
teak country of Blora?

543
00:32:21.580 --> 00:32:23.000
A dharma with the lid off.

544
00:32:23.580 --> 00:32:27.180
No prophet on a mountain, no book handed
down from the sky,

545
00:32:27.220 --> 00:32:30.160
no two-thousand-year pedigree to make it
untouchable.

546
00:32:30.760 --> 00:32:32.980
Just an illiterate farmer and his
neighbours,

547
00:32:33.060 --> 00:32:36.220
in a squeezed and unjust corner of a
colonised island,

548
00:32:36.240 --> 00:32:37.980
deciding (deliberately,

549
00:32:37.980 --> 00:32:39.760
against the grain of their own culture

550
00:32:39.880 --> 00:32:43.120
and the full weight of an empire) how they
were going to live.

551
00:32:43.920 --> 00:32:47.360
And then living it so completely, and
teaching it so well,

552
00:32:47.360 --> 00:32:49.660
with nothing but their voices and their
feet,

553
00:32:49.860 --> 00:32:53.520
that the way outlasted the man by a
century and walked itself

554
00:32:53.520 --> 00:32:57.140
from a colonial tax revolt into an
environmental protest

555
00:32:57.400 --> 00:32:59.140
without ever losing its shape.

556
00:33:01.040 --> 00:33:04.500
That is the thing the great traditions can
no longer easily show us,

557
00:33:04.580 --> 00:33:06.320
and the wong sikep can.

558
00:33:06.900 --> 00:33:10.400
We are used to thinking of a dharma as
something ancient and given,

559
00:33:10.560 --> 00:33:13.920
something you are born into or convert to,
something you find.

560
00:33:14.520 --> 00:33:17.620
The Sedulur Sikep let us watch the
opposite happen.

561
00:33:18.280 --> 00:33:20.480
A dharma is something a community makes,

562
00:33:20.600 --> 00:33:24.360
and then keeps, by choosing it, naming it,
and saying,

563
00:33:24.460 --> 00:33:26.740
over and over, with their feet in the
cement

564
00:33:26.820 --> 00:33:29.400
if it comes to that: this is who we are.

565
00:33:31.240 --> 00:33:33.160
If even one small, poor,

566
00:33:33.160 --> 00:33:35.800
unlettered community in the forests of
Java could

567
00:33:35.960 --> 00:33:38.600
build a whole ethical world without
scripture,

568
00:33:38.600 --> 00:33:43.100
clergy, or institutional machinery, and
defend it for a hundred years,

569
00:33:43.140 --> 00:33:45.560
then dharmas are not relics we inherit.

570
00:33:46.020 --> 00:33:47.700
They are things people make.

571
00:33:48.200 --> 00:33:51.860
Which means that, eventually, they are
things we could make, too.

572
00:33:53.940 --> 00:33:56.080
But the wong sikep are only one.

573
00:33:56.620 --> 00:33:58.920
Having seen a dharma assembled this
clearly,

574
00:33:58.980 --> 00:34:02.220
in this much close-up, you start to notice
them everywhere,

575
00:34:02.240 --> 00:34:03.640
and they are not all alike:

576
00:34:03.780 --> 00:34:07.520
a single South African word that contains
an entire theory of the self;

577
00:34:07.760 --> 00:34:09.760
a Roman emperor's private notebook;

578
00:34:09.960 --> 00:34:12.720
a song that maps a thousand kilometres of
desert;

579
00:34:12.960 --> 00:34:17.640
a promise made in another desert, half a
world away, that founded three religions.

580
00:34:18.240 --> 00:34:20.260
The teak forest was one country.

581
00:34:20.540 --> 00:34:22.704
Next, we cross into the others.

